# Database migrations

Run Alembic from `backend/` so the default relative SQLite path and `.env` resolve
to the intended app instance:

```bash
uv run alembic current
uv run alembic upgrade head
```

`DATABASE_URL` selects SQLite or PostgreSQL. Migrations update the journal schema;
DuckDB tables are initialized separately by the warehouse layer.

## Revisions

| Revision | Changes |
|---|---|
| `0001_initial_double_entry` | Journal entries/lines, accounts, payees, salary profiles, budgets, and fund overrides. |
| `0002_fortnightly` (head) | Fortnightly salary period: PostgreSQL enum gains `FORTNIGHTLY`; SQLite column widens from 7 to 11 characters. Existing monthly/annual values and breakdown snapshots are preserved. |

The fortnightly downgrade refuses to proceed while any profile uses that period.
Back up and remove those profiles, or replace them with correctly recalculated
monthly/annual profiles, before downgrading. Merely selecting a different active
profile does not change the saved period of a fortnightly profile.

On PostgreSQL, downgrade recreates the earlier enum after checking for fortnightly
rows. On SQLite, it restores the earlier column length. Test this against a backup
before downgrading a populated database.

## Validation status

`tests/integration/test_salary_migration.py` exercises SQLite upgrade/downgrade,
snapshot preservation, and the downgrade guard using a disposable database.
PostgreSQL upgrade SQL was generated and inspected on 2026-11-07; execution against
a live PostgreSQL instance has not been verified.

Migrations do not populate demo data or clear financial records. For initialization
and operational reset details, see [the walkthrough](../../WALKTHROUGH.md) and
[UI/UX testing](../../docs/UI_UX_TESTING.md).
